# {{displayName}}

An Expo app with [Tula Auth](https://github.com/Tula-Solutions), and the Tula API it signs in
to, which runs on your machine in Docker. The server's files are here, at the project's root;
the app is in `app/`, a copy of the Tula repository's Expo example (`examples/expo/app`).

**Nobody has run this app on a phone, in a simulator or in an emulator**: the example it is
copied from was installed, typechecked and bundled, and no build of it was completed. Read
"What was run, and what was not" in the Tula repository's `examples/expo/README.md` before
you count on any of it.

## Start the server

```sh
bun install
bunx tula dev        # starts the stack, migrates, seeds, mints development keys
```

`tula dev` prints the URLs: the API, its reference, and Mailpit, the inbox every email the
API sends lands in (verification codes included). It writes the development keys to
`.env.local` here; run it again any time, it changes nothing that is already there.

## Start the app

```sh
cd app
bun install
cp .env.example .env.local
```

Fill in `app/.env.local`. Both values are public (Expo puts every `EXPO_PUBLIC_*` variable
into the app's bundle), and neither is written for you:

- `EXPO_PUBLIC_TULA_PUBLISHABLE_KEY`: the value of `TULA_PUBLISHABLE_KEY` in the project's
  `.env.local`. **Never the secret key.**
- `EXPO_PUBLIC_TULA_API_URL`: the API as the app reaches it. The iOS simulator shares this
  machine's `http://localhost:{{apiPort}}`; the Android emulator reaches it as
  `http://10.0.2.2:{{apiPort}}`. A phone reaches neither: see "On a phone" below.

The app imports native modules Expo Go does not contain (a passkey sheet, Google's sheet, a
device key), so it needs a development build, made once:

```sh
bunx expo run:ios        # needs Xcode; or
bunx expo run:android    # needs Android Studio's SDK and an emulator
bun run start            # afterwards
```

## Tula's own screens

This app draws its screens itself, on `@tula-tech/expo`'s headless client. Tula's native screens
(SwiftUI on iOS, Compose on Android) are a separate package, `@tula-tech/expo-screens`, with a
native module of its own: nothing here installs it, so your build compiles none of it. It is
a separate install and an app uses it instead of the headless client, never beside it. Its
iOS module has not been compiled, and no screen has been drawn through it anywhere: read
`docs/expo.md` in the Tula repository before you add it.

## On a phone

A phone cannot reach `localhost`, and a passkey, an app link and a provider's own sheet need
`https` on a name the phone trusts. From the project's root:

```sh
bunx tula dev --tunnel   # the stack under this machine's Tailscale Funnel address
```

It needs your own Tailscale account, signed in on this machine, and it prints the address
and the settings it did not change. Put the address in `app/.env.local` as
`EXPO_PUBLIC_TULA_API_URL` and build the app onto the phone (`bunx expo run:ios --device`,
`bunx expo run:android --device`). Close the tunnel with `bunx tula dev down`. Nobody has
run `tula dev --tunnel` against a phone.

## What needs your own accounts

| For | You need | Where it goes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| A build on an iPhone | An Apple Developer account: a team, a bundle ID of your own in `app/app.json` (it is `com.example.tula` here) | Xcode's signing settings |
| Passkeys | A public `https` domain (the tunnel's will do), and the app registered under `nativeApps` in `tula.config.ts`: on iOS your team ID and bundle ID, on Android your package name and the SHA-256 fingerprint of your signing certificate | `tula.config.ts`, then `bunx tula apply`; `EXPO_PUBLIC_TULA_DOMAIN` in `app/.env.local` |
| Sign in with Apple's sheet | An Apple Developer account with the Sign in with Apple capability for your bundle ID | Apple's developer site; the Apple provider and the iOS app in `tula.config.ts` |
| Sign in with Google's sheet | A Google Cloud project of your own: a web OAuth client ID, and an iOS or Android client ID (Android's is made from your package name and SHA-256 fingerprint) | `EXPO_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_WEB_CLIENT_ID`, `EXPO_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_IOS_CLIENT_ID`; the Google provider in `tula.config.ts` |
| A provider in the system browser | The provider's own OAuth client, and the app's redirect URL (`com.example.tula:/oauth/callback` until you change the scheme) among the environment's allowed redirect URLs | `tula.config.ts` |
| The tunnel | A Tailscale account | `tailscale up` on this machine |

None of these is made for you, and no identifier here is yours: change `com.example.tula`
in `app/app.json` and in `app/src/tula.ts` before you register anything.

## What is here

| File | What |
| --- | --- |
| `app/` | The Expo app: its own `package.json`, `.env.example` and `.gitignore`. |
| `compose.yaml` | The local stack: Postgres, Redis, Mailpit, the migrations and the Tula API. |
| `.env` | The stack's secrets, generated for this project: the master key, the instance admin token, the database passwords. Ignored by git. **Back up `TULA_MASTER_KEY`**: without it the stored signing keys and provider credentials cannot be opened. |
| `.env.local` | Written by `tula dev`: the API URL and the development keys. Ignored by git. |
| `.env.example` | What `.env` holds, without the values. Commit this one. |
| `tula.config.ts` | The environment's settings as code. |
| `docker/postgres/init.sh` | Creates the API's database role when the volume is first created. |

## Everyday commands

```sh
bunx tula doctor          # checks the stack: database, migrations, master key, mail, Redis
bunx tula diff            # what tula.config.ts would change on the server
bunx tula apply           # make the server match tula.config.ts
bunx tula dev down        # stop the stack, and close a tunnel (the data is kept)
```

## The API image

`TULA_API_IMAGE` in `.env` names the Tula API image; it is `{{apiImage}}` here. A released
image (`ghcr.io/tula-solutions/tula-api:<version>`) is pulled the first time the stack
starts. To run an API built from the Tula repository instead, build it there under a tag of
your own and name that tag in `.env`:

```sh
docker build -f apps/api/Dockerfile -t tula-api:local .   # in the Tula repository
```

The API is on port `{{apiPort}}` and Mailpit on `{{mailpitPort}}` (`API_PORT` and
`MAILPIT_UI_PORT` in `.env`).
